From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 20:21:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Concurrent-serving sweep tooling for vLLM (not yet run)

The committed matrix is batch size 1, which never exercises continuous
batching. run_load_sweep.sh starts one vllm serve process and runs vLLM's own
`vllm bench serve` at --max-concurrency 1/4/16/64 with --request-rate inf,
three repeats, on the matrix's fixed prompt (128 forced output tokens, so the
concurrency-1 cell lines up with the committed vLLM row) and on random-length
prompts (the fixed prompt runs in synchronized waves under a cap).

vLLM 0.4.2's benchmark_serving.py has only --request-rate (checked in the
v0.4.2 source), so the sweep uses a current vLLM in its own venv
(setup_vllm_serve.sh, 0.34.0) and is reported as a separate measurement.
load_sweep_table.py turns the result JSONs into the README table and lists
incomplete runs instead of dropping them; CI parses the fixture results and
syntax-checks the shell. Nothing has run on a GPU; the README says so and
gives the cost plan (about one A100 hour).

