+## Concurrent serving sweep (tooling committed, not yet run)
+
+The limitation above that matters most for vLLM is the batch-size-1 workload. `scripts/run_load_sweep.sh` measures the serving case: one `vllm serve` process, then vLLM's own `vllm bench serve` client at a fixed cap of 1, 4, 16 and 64 in-flight requests with all requests queued at the start (`--request-rate inf --max-concurrency N`, the pattern vLLM's benchmarking guide calls "maximum throughput"). Three repeats per level. Two prompt sets: the matrix's single prompt repeated with exactly 128 output tokens, so that on the same GPU type as the committed matrix (A100 40 GB SXM4) the concurrency-1 cell lines up with the committed vLLM row, and on any other GPU it is the sweep's own reference point, and random prompts (input 256, output 128, both ±50%) because identical requests under a concurrency cap run in synchronized waves and each request's time to first token then includes the whole wave's prefill. Reported per level: requests/s, output tokens/s, and p50/p99 of time to first token, time per output token and end-to-end latency, with per-request detail saved.
+
+The pinned vLLM 0.4.2 cannot run this: its `benchmark_serving.py` had only `--request-rate` and no concurrency cap (checked in the v0.4.2 source). The sweep uses a current vLLM in its own venv (`scripts/setup_vllm_serve.sh`, 0.34.0 at the time of writing) and is a separate measurement from the 0.4.2 rows; the two must not share a table. Analysis: `python analysis/load_sweep_table.py --results-dir results/load_sweep` (parse check in CI on `tests/fixtures/load_sweep/`).
+
+Plan for the run: setup about 15 min, 24 client runs of 128 prompts about 25 min, so about 1 GPU hour; cap 5 USD (3.9 h on a Lambda A10 at 1.29 USD/h, 2.5 h on an A100 40 GB at 1.99, live prices 2026-11-27). The GPU is recorded in `results/load_sweep/ENVIRONMENT_serve.md`; a 24 GB A10 holds the fp16 weights (about 14.5 GB) with room for the 4k-context KV cache. Nothing here has been executed on a GPU; the scripts were syntax-checked and the analysis was run on fixtures only.
+
+```bash
+scripts/setup_vllm_serve.sh
+scripts/run_load_sweep.sh --out results/load_sweep_smoke --repeats 1 --concurrency "1 4" --num-prompts 8
+scripts/run_load_sweep.sh --out results/load_sweep --repeats 3
+python3 analysis/load_sweep_table.py --results-dir results/load_sweep
+```
+
 ## Install
 
 Python 3.11 and a CUDA-capable environment are expected for the configured GPU runs.
